  "base_commit": "4ab865a848a1da3d10fb674f857e5fff13094644",
  "pr_number": 15634,
  "issue_number": 15632,
  "issue_title": "Component update is skipped when object v-bind changes keys and the new prop value is undefined",
  "issue_body": "### Vue version\n\n3.5.38\n\n### Link to minimal reproduction\n\nhttps://play.vuejs.org/#\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Open the reproduction.\n2. Notice that both child components initially display \"Add at least one item\".\n3. Click \"Add item\".\n4. The click handler adds an item and schedules the counter update using `nextTick()`.\n5. Wait for the updates to complete.\n6. Observe that `items` contains one item, `hasErrors` is `false`, and `counter` is `1`.\n7. Compare the results of the \"Dynamic object shape\" and \"Stable object shape\" components.\n\n### What is expected?\n\nBoth child components should clear the previous `errorMessage` prop and display:\n\n```text\nCount: 1\n```\n\nWhen the object passed through `v-bind` changes from:\n\n```js\n{\n  errorMessage: 'Add at least one item',\n}\n```\n\nto:\n\n```js\n{\n  counter: undefined,\n}\n```\n\nthe removal of `errorMessage` should be detected even though the new prop value is `undefined`.\n\nThe watcher for `props.errorMessage` should be triggered with the previous error message and `undefined` as the new value.\n\n### What is actually happening?\n\nThe component using the stable-shaped binding works correctly: it clears `errorMessage` and displays:\n\n```text\nCount: 1\n```\n\nThe component using the dynamically shaped binding receives the updated counter but retains the stale `errorMessage`.\n\nIts effective props become:\n\n```js\n{\n  errorMessage: 'Add at least one item',\n  counter: 1,\n}\n```\n\nAs a result, it continues to display:\n\n```text\nAdd at least one item\n```\n\nThe computed binding in the parent is recalculated correctly, but the watcher for `props.errorMessage` in the child is not triggered because the stale prop is never cleared.\n\n### System Info\n\n```shell\nReproduces in the Vue SFC Playground using Chrome.\n```\n\n### Any additional comments?\n\nThe reproduction uses `nextTick()` to intentionally split the state change into two render cycles:\n\n```js\nconst addItem = () => {\n  items.value.push('Item');\n\n  nextTick(() => counter.value = items.value.length);\n};\n```\n\nThis produces the following props transitions:\n\n```js\n// Initial render\n{\n  errorMessage: 'Add at least one item',\n}\n\n// First render after adding an item\n{\n  counter: undefined,\n}\n\n// Second render after nextTick()\n{\n  counter: 1,\n}\n```\n\nDuring the first update, the previous and next props objects contain the same number of keys:\n\n```js\nconst prevProps = {\n  errorMessage: 'Add at least one item',\n};\n\nconst nextProps = {\n  counter: undefined,\n};\n\nObject.keys(prevProps).length === Object.keys(nextProps).length;\n// true\n```\n\nWhen Vue checks the `counter` key from `nextProps`, the comparison is effectively:\n\n```js\nnextProps.counter === prevProps.counter;\n// undefined === undefined\n// true\n```\n\n`counter` is not present in `prevProps`, but reading a missing property also returns `undefined`. Because the comparison checks the values without checking property presence, Vue considers the props unchanged\nand skips the child component update.\n\nThe child component therefore retains:\n\n```js\n{\n  errorMessage: 'Add at least one item',\n}\n```\n\nHowever, after the skipped update, the component VNode has already advanced to:\n\n```js\n{\n  counter: undefined,\n}\n```\n\nWhen `counter` is changed to `1` after `nextTick()`, Vue detects that update and patches the child. At this point, the previous VNode no longer contains `errorMessage`, so the stale `errorMessage` stored in\nthe component instance is not cleared.\n\nThe child consequently ends up with both the stale error and the new counter:\n\n```js\n{\n  errorMessage: 'Add at least one item',\n  counter: 1,\n}\n```\n\nThe stable-shaped version avoids the issue because Vue can directly detect the `errorMessage` value change:\n\n```js\nconst stableBind = computed(() => ({\n  errorMessage: hasErrors.value\n    ? 'Add at least one item'\n    : undefined,\n  counter: counter.value,\n}));\n```\n\nA possible fix may be to account for property presence when comparing props, in addition to comparing their values:\n\n```js\n!hasOwn(prevProps, key) ||\nhasPropValueChanged(nextProps, prevProps, key)\n```\n\nObject-style `v-bind` is documented as equivalent to passing the object properties individually. The documentation does not state that the bound object must retain the same set of keys:\nhttps://vuejs.org/guide/components/props.html#binding-multiple-properties-using-an-object",
